   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">National mechanism for amicable settlement of labour disputes revitalised:

Tripartite Working Group (TWG) was established by Order of the Minister of Finance and Economy no. 233, dated 25.09.2020 &quot;On the establishment of the
working group for improving the public procedures of mediation and conciliation of labour disputes&quot;. The main focus was to revise and streamline the public procedures for mediation and conciliation of labour disputes, and to formulate some initial thoughts on how best to facilitate access to justice in labour disputes for all, including vulnerable workers.

The tripartite discussions within the Working Group and focus groups, produced a report with a set of recommendations, a comprehensive draft by-law on the functioning of mediation and conciliation structures, and relevant procedures, and a practical guide for mediators/conciliators on how to conduct effective mediation and conciliation.

In its meeting of 16 February 2021 the National Labour Council discussed and endorsed the draft by-law that was formally adopted in May (Instruction of the Minister of Finance and Economy no.13 dated 12.05.2021). With the support of Sweden, during 2020 the ILO provided technical assistance to the Ministry of Finance and Economy to review and streamline existing public mediation and conciliation procedures.

The ILO assisted the development and operationalazing of the Labour dispute management information system established (LDMIS), provided legal and technical support in drafting the Government act on operationalising the LDMIS and in streamlining mediation and conciliation procedures. It also provided LDMIS User training.

The ILO provided continuous up skilling programme for mediators and judges.

The ILO developed and shared a guide for conducting mediation and conciliation, and a Manual for handling labour disputes in the workplace.

The ILO developed a methodology for the independent assessment of industrial relations and conducted the assessments in 20 companies and Action Plans for prevention and resolution of Labour Disputes adopted.</narrative>
   </description>
